  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) covers the `kibana_sample_data_flights` index. It has 33 fields, including `Carrier`, `Cancelled`, `AvgTicketPrice`, `Dest`, `Origin` and `FlightDelay`. Its time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-91bd13f1 Flights\" (id `c888054c-be9c-42b5-a766-7694ede5472c`) has exactly two panels on that data view. One is a line chart of flight count over time. The other is a bar chart of flight count by `Carrier`.\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-91bd13f1 Cancelled flights\" (id `e98f4fa5-794f-4d36-b6e5-1e29a14ba242`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 documents with `Cancelled: true` arrive in the last 5 minutes. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** The carrier panel's title is now \"Flights by carrier\", confirmed in the update response. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true` with a 1m schedule.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
